<LabLayout title="Operations method · Tether Lab" description="Finite manifests, capacity versus fixed-window dispatch, inbound momentum exchange and an explicit energy and angular-momentum ledger." active="operations">
<main class="guide" id="lab-content"><header class="guide-header"><p class="micro">OPERATIONS GUIDE / OPS-0.1.0</p><h1>A service is more than two throws.</h1><p>Track a finite list of shipments through one continuous facility trajectory. Arrival times, transfers, losses and unused inventory stay in the record.</p><a class="button-link" href="/lab/operations/">Open Operations →</a></header>
<div class="guide-layout"><nav class="guide-nav" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#dispatch">Dispatch rules</a><a href="#inbound">Inbound cargo</a><a href="#balance">Energy & momentum</a><a href="#scope">Limits & references</a></nav><div class="guide-body">
<section class="guide-section" id="dispatch"><h2>Fixed windows are not fixed incoming orbits.</h2><p><strong>Capacity mode</strong> uses a finite ordered queue and seeks the next qualifying encounter. The displayed window times are ignored. It measures this scheduler’s opportunities, not an optimized maximum cadence.</p><p><strong>Scheduled mode</strong> uses the capture-window centers and half-width specified before propagation. It does not move a missed shipment to a later window. The windows are fixed, but the arrival orbits are still constructed after an opportunity is reserved. This does not demonstrate navigation from a pre-existing launch orbit.</p><p>Planning is attempted on a deterministic 60-second clock and when the outbound readiness hold is first satisfied. A coast forecast seeks the next lower radial pass for outbound loads, or upper radial pass for inbound loads. Every accepted example includes 90 seconds of independently propagated approach and numerical position/velocity checks. Recovery is suspended while a reserved coast forecast and encounter play out.</p><p>Outbound pickups require the existing radius, radial speed, tangential speed and spin tolerances, including a 60-second hold after the initial supplied encounter. Inbound pickups do not require the reference orbit to have been restored first; they are intended to exchange momentum with a depleted facility. Both directions still require valid capture residuals and the full structural/clearance checks.</p><p>The scheduler is sequential, not a global optimizer. Head-of-queue blocking is possible. An unmatched or out-of-window rendezvous does not attach a payload. An unserved shipment is not a missed window in capacity mode. A model limit stops the facility; unreached rows remain in the results.</p></section>
<section class="guide-section" id="inbound"><h2>The returning shipment is another physical object.</h2><p>An inbound payload is captured near the upper radial position, carried through the chosen Earth-relative rotation angle, and released with the tip’s actual position and velocity. Its mass, energy and angular momentum are included while attached. No inventory or momentum credits are created from tonnage alone.</p><p>A counted outbound delivery must gain energy, remain bound, have perigee ≥120 km and meet its minimum apogee. A counted inbound return must lose energy, remain bound, have perigee ≥120 km and meet its maximum apogee. A release that misses a destination target still physically exchanges energy and momentum; its transfer is not erased.</p><p>Both the manifest mass and release phase matter. A half-turn inbound release can intersect Earth. This model does not substitute a safe delivery or treat that outcome as successful atmospheric entry.</p><p>With assistance selected, the controller either runs whenever the tether is unoccupied or defers while the next queued row is inbound. Power and propellant are not replenished. The defer policy can itself cause missed windows; it is a choice to test, not an optimal logistics law.</p></section>
<section class="guide-section" id="balance"><h2>Keep the state history separate from the transfer ledger.</h2><p>The history curves track the same <strong>dry facility</strong> throughout: strength tether, terminals, hub and any selected electrical hardware. They exclude the attached payload and propellant, avoiding artificial history jumps when a cargo is counted in the facility mass.</p><p>The conservation ledger separately includes the complete instantaneous facility inventory, including fuel and attached cargo. At a matched capture, the payload’s instantaneous energy and angular momentum enter that inventory. At release they leave. Powered intervals integrate actual actuator work and external angular impulse. Signed energy and angular momentum carried by removed hub propellant are also accounted for.</p><pre class="equation-panel" tabindex="0">ΔE_inventory = E_captured − E_released + W_actuator − E_shed_propellant
ΔH_inventory = H_captured − H_released + J_actuator − H_shed_propellant

Payload energy gain = mass × (specific energy at release − at capture)
Payload angular gain = mass × (specific angular momentum at release − at capture)</pre><p>Negative payload gain transfers that quantity back to the facility. Equal total mass in and out does not imply equal energy or angular-momentum exchange. The numerical residuals test these identities; they are not safety margins.</p><p>The <a href="/lab/method/#electrodynamic">E0 electrical ledger</a> retains its bus input, environment exchange, mechanical work and dissipation terms. The <strong>zero-power, same-hardware</strong> control retains conductor and electrical hardware mass. Choosing None instead removes E0-specific hardware, and is not the same controlled comparison.</p><p>Resource comparisons are not rankings unless destination orbits, transferred mass, inbound supply, schedule and observation duration are comparable. A finite successful manifest does not prove indefinite repeatability.</p></section>
<section class="guide-section" id="scope"><h2>Longer observation, the same limited mechanics.</h2><p>OPS-0.1 uses the unchanged D1p-0.4 spherical-Earth, planar rigid-body mechanics with 48 strength-tether cells by default. The integration step defaults to 2 seconds. Event boundaries are located independently of replay speed. The manifest is limited to 20 shipments and the observation to 24 hours.</p><p>No ascent, autonomous rendezvous guidance, capture shock, elastic waves, debris, atmosphere, thermal lifetime, maintenance, eclipse, batteries, three-dimensional field model, T4 dynamics or lunar propagation is added here. A released particle is tracked until the local 120 km cutoff; its later atmospheric motion is not invented. Operations is versioned separately from Flight Studio.</p><p>The Earth–Moon transport study by Hoyt and collaborators discusses restoring tether energy through return traffic: <a href="https://www.niac.usra.edu/files/studies/final_report/7Hoyt.pdf">Cislunar Tether Transport System, 1999, introduction and Appendix L</a>. It motivates this experiment but does not validate our Earth-only scheduler or its return destinations.</p><p>The older <a href="/lab/">two-shipment Flight Studio</a>, its saved designs and numerical tests remain separate. Operations shares physical functions, not successful-flight answers.</p><a class="button-link" href="/lab/operations/">Back to your manifest →</a></section>
</div></div></main></LabLayout>
